+# The C DECLARATION TYPE for each name in `_DISPATCH_TABLE_GLOBAL_NAMES` above.
+# The dict/set split the four hand-written copies each carried as two separate
+# name lists, written once as the per-name type it always was.
+#
+# IT MUST COVER EVERY NAME IN THE TUPLE. The importer's declaration site
+# declares each of these as a field of the importing module's own globals
+# struct, and the generated code READS it back through that field, so dropping
+# a name here does not merely stop forcing its type -- it stops declaring the
+# field, and the reference becomes `'struct _mojo_middle_exprtypes_toplev' has
+# no member named '_CPP_CALLABLE_CTYPE'`. Measured: narrowing this to the eight
+# names the four copies originally agreed on took the self-host build from 35
+# gcc errors to 99.
+#
+# Every TYPE here has to be the one the global's own definition has, because the
+# answer lands in the SHARED bare-name-keyed `_global_c_decl_types` and
+# `_own_overlay_global_ctype`'s rule 1 ("a container cdecl beats a scalar
+# own-conclusion") then lets it beat the OWNING module's own correct
+# conclusion. A blanket `MojoDict *` — which is what the two sites used when
+# this was a name list — therefore re-typed every `frozenset` below to a dict:
+#
+#     # ERROR: compiling imported module 'module_loader': cannot coerce
+#     #   MojoSet * to MojoDict * (incompatible container kinds)
+#
+# for four modules of the self-host closure, `selfhost` red, exit 1.
+#
+# `'int64_t'` is the BOX: the value is right and there is no typed accessor,
+# which is the convention `_gscan_declare_global`'s own non-dispatch arms use
+# for exactly this case. Read the definition before adding a name — a `{...}`
+# literal with no `key: value` pairs is a SET, which is why `_CMP_OPS` is
+# `MojoSet *` despite the name (`generated_dispatch.py` declares
+# `_CMP_OPS: set`) and why `_STR_RETURNING_METHODS` is a set and not a dict.
